@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Studios: Every argument)

Every studio argument and theme, light and mode value that the other studio files do not already use, each in one small working example.

@section(Parametric 3-D parts)

light=studio is the three-point light that follows the camera (the default).

@cad(light=studio)
cube([10, 10, 10]);
@end

light=technical is flat and even, for reading dimensions.

@cad(light=technical)
cylinder(h=10, r=5);
@end

light=dramatic is a hard key and a strong rim, for a figure in a deck.

@cad(light=dramatic)
sphere(r=8);
@end

light=sun is fixed in the world, so turning the part shows how the light falls.

@cad(light=sun)
cube([20, 10, 5]);
@end

theme=dark gives the studio the graphite interface.

@cad(theme=dark)
cube([10, 10, 10]);
@end

theme=studio is the same graphite interface under its other name.

@cad(theme=studio)
cube([10, 10, 10]);
@end

theme=showcase adds glow and glass, for a demo.

@cad(theme=showcase)
sphere(r=10);
@end

output=right puts the viewport beside the code instead of below it.

@cad(output=right)
cube([10, 10, 20]);
@end

submit=server lets a student hand the exercise in; id= tells the submissions apart.

@cad(id=block1, submit=server, expect="volume=1000")
cube([10, 10, 10]);
@end

noedit hides the source editor and shows only the part.

@cad(noedit)
translate([0, 0, 5]) sphere(r=5);
@end

x=, y= and z= place the card absolutely, and z= puts it in front of or behind its neighbours.

@cad(x=0, y=0, z=1)
cube([8, 8, 8]);
@end

benchmark= names a value that receives the build time in ms.

@cad(benchmark=cadtime)
cube([10, 10, 10]);
@end

@section(Meshes and models)

h= sets the mesh editor's height.

@mesh(h=300)
@end

theme=studio on the mesh editor.

@mesh(theme=studio)
@end

theme=showcase on the mesh editor.

@mesh(theme=showcase)
@end

shade=smooth shades a model smoothly.

@model(src=cad-3d.assets/bracket.obj, shade=smooth)

shade=wire draws a model as a wireframe.

@model(src=cad-3d.assets/bracket.obj, shade=wire)

@section(Calculators and compute)

mode=hp48 is the four-level RPN calculator in the style of the HP 48.

@calc(mode=hp48)

float=left wraps the text on the left of the calculator; h= sets its height.

@calc(scientific, float=left, w=280, h=360)

The calculator floats beside this paragraph, and the text keeps flowing around it until it runs past the bottom of the calculator.

preload=no fetches the Octo engine only on the first run instead of as the page opens.

@settings(module=octo, preload=no)

theme=dark on a spreadsheet.

@spreadsheet(theme=dark)
Item	Qty
Pens	3
Pads	2
@end

theme=showcase on a spreadsheet.

@spreadsheet(theme=showcase)
Item	Qty
Pens	3
Pads	2
@end

@section(Algorithms and automata)

theme=dark on the algorithm player. It takes no pts=: the linter says pts= has no effect here, because a student cannot change the program.

@algoviz(theme=dark)
algo: insertion
data: 8 2 5 1
@end

theme=studio on an automaton.

@automaton(theme=studio)
type: dfa
start: q0
accept: q1
q0 -1-> q1
q1 -1-> q0
@end

theme=showcase on an automaton.

@automaton(theme=showcase)
type: dfa
start: q0
accept: q0
q0 -0-> q0
q0 -1-> q1
q1 -1-> q0
@end

@section(Science)

theme=dark on the chemistry studio.

@chem(theme=dark)
CCO
@end

theme=showcase on the chemistry studio.

@chem(theme=showcase)
H2 + O2 -> H2O
@end

theme=studio on a physics simulation.

@sim(theme=studio)
system: pendulum
length: 1.0
@end

theme=showcase on a physics simulation.

@sim(theme=showcase)
system: spring
@end

theme=studio on a quantum circuit.

@quantum(theme=studio)
q = QUBITS(1)
H(q)
@end

theme=showcase on a quantum circuit.

@quantum(theme=showcase)
q0, q1 = QUBITS(2)
H(q0)
@end

@section(Drawing, music and cards)

theme=studio on the drawing studio.

@draw(theme=studio, height=200)
rect r1 at 40,40 size 120,70
@end

theme=showcase on the drawing studio.

@draw(theme=showcase, height=200)
ellipse e1 at 100,80 size 80,50
@end

theme=studio on the music studio.

@music(theme=studio)
C4:q E4:q G4:h
@end

theme=showcase on the music studio.

@music(theme=showcase)
G4:q E4:q C4:h
@end

theme=studio on a flashcard deck.

@flashcards(id=cards-studio, theme=studio)
Unit of charge :: the coulomb
@end

theme=showcase on a flashcard deck.

@flashcards(id=cards-showcase, theme=showcase)
Unit of power :: the watt
@end

@section(Circuits)

theme=studio on a schematic.

@EECad(schematic, theme=studio)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
R1 resistor 1k at 30,40
GND ground at 10,5
@end

theme=showcase on a schematic.

@EECad(schematic, theme=showcase)
V1 vsource 5V at 10,40
R1 resistor 1k at 30,40
GND ground at 10,5
@end

labels=no hides part names on the breadboard (the default, written out).

@EECad(sourdough, labels=no)
@end

pinlabels=yes shows a board's own lettering (the default, written out).

@EECad(sourdough, board=uno, pinlabels=yes)
@end

grid=yes shows the dot grid (the default, written out).

@EECad(sourdough, grid=yes)
@end

views=both offers the Breadboard and Schematic tabs only.

@EECad(sourdough, views=both)
@end

views=breadboard offers the bench only.

@EECad(sourdough, views=breadboard)
@end

hex= carries a compiled sketch; a publication writes it, never an author, so this one is an invented placeholder.

@EECad(sourdough, board=uno, hex=00)
@end

@section(Proofs)

The proof arguments goal=, pts=, partial, auto, bool and rat are written as @@lab(prove, …), the only spelling there is, and each is shown in science.idoc.
